{"qid":"Q126936","names":["Visigothic Kingdom"],"title":"Visigothic Kingdom","endonym":{"native":"Gutþiuda Þiudinassus","lang":"got"},"capsule":"The Visigothic Kingdom was a Germanic successor state that evolved from the Visigothic settlement in Aquitaine (412 AD) granted by Rome, with its capital at Toulouse until the Frankish king Clovis defeated Alaric II at Vouillé (507 AD) and drove the Visigoths into Hispania. There, centred on Toledo from the mid-6th century, the Visigoths consolidated rule over the entire Iberian Peninsula by 624 when Suintila expelled the last Byzantine enclave. King Reccared I converted the Visigothic court from Arianism to Nicene Christianity at the Third Council of Toledo (589), unifying it with the Hispano-Roman population. The kingdom collapsed abruptly: a civil war between rival claimants invited a Muslim Umayyad army into Iberia; Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed the strait in 711 and defeated and killed King Roderic at the Battle of Guadalete, and the Umayyad conquest was largely complete by 721.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Visigothic Kingdom's territorial control over Hispania is well attested by church council records, law codes, and numismatic evidence; the northern Basque/Cantabrian fringe was never effectively administered, and maps showing full Iberian coverage overstate actual Visigothic authority.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Visigoth","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Visigoth"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Visigothic Kingdom","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visigothic_Kingdom"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Visigothic King